t B at 72%) all land in the same cell.\nSTEP 4, B = residual = 100 - 12 - 22 - 22 = 44. B absorbs growth 1.7-2.5% (0.33) plus the low-growth/moderate-inflation leftover cell the literal definitions leave uncovered. B being modal while my CQ3 P50 sits at 1.6% is coherent precisely BECAUSE of the residual clause: a 1.6% world with core inflation in the 2.0-2.7% band is neither C nor D and falls to B.\nCQ2 CONSISTENCY CHECK PERFORMED: my CQ2 = 43 requires OECD core >=2.50% on a 2027-36 average at 66% marginal. That is compatible with putting only 22 on C, because C needs the tighter >=2.7% threshold on the LATER, cooler 2032-36 sub-window AND simultaneous growth <2.0%; the conjunction, not the inflation level, is what keeps C at 22. SUM = 100.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:55.690Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Atlanta Fed Wage Growth Tracker industry series: the \\\"construction and mining\\\" 12-month median minus the \\\"finance and business services\\\" 12-month median, read as a rolling 12-month average. Currently I could NOT retrieve either industry series' 2026 level - the overall tracker was 3.9% in September 2026, down from 4.1% in August (release dated October 8 2026), with job-stayers 3.6% -> 3.4% and job-changers 5.0% -> 4.7%; methodology caveat verbatim: \\\"The tracker measures the median wage change for matched individuals, which is not the same as growth in the median wage.\\\" Falsifier: that spread sustaining +1.5pp or more for eight consecutive quarters.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ1 moves UP from 14 to roughly 26-30 (conjunct B's marginal from 20% to ~40%): it would mean the F+Q wage signal is beating the J+K+M median-composition effect head-on, which is the exact mechanism I argued cannot happen. It also moves CQ6-B up ~5 at the expense of D, and hypothesis plank (a) up from 6 to ~20.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Philippine BSP cash remittances and IBPAP/CXAP IT-BPM headcount, read TOGETHER rather than separately. Current: remittances record $35.634B in 2025, +3.3% from $34.493B; $14.1B Jan-May 2026; BSP forecast $36.6B for 2026 (+2.7%). CC-BPM employment 1.68M in 2025 (+4% from 1.62M), projected ~1.73M in 2026; IBPAP midterm range widened to 1.85-2.14M with \\\"its own communications flag the 2026 figures as under review\\\". Falsifier of MY view: an outright year-on-year DECLINE in CC-BPM headcount of 3% or more while remittances also decline year-on-year.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Hypothesis plank (b) moves UP from 14 to roughly 45 - it would mean the virtual leg is contracting in levels (not just in elasticity) AND the physical/remittance leg is failing to offset, which is the specific conjunction my 14 denies. It also moves CQ6-D up ~4 (a BIS-style demand deficit transmitted through the global South) and adds ~2 to AI-Q5's implied coupled value.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"GDP-weighted OECD-38 core CPI/PCE, 12-month rate. Currently OECD-wide core is 3.6%, \\\"stable for the third consecutive month\\\" (OECD release, 6 October 2026), headline 4.3% in August 2026, euro area core 2.5% in September 2026 (up from 2.4%), energy inflation above 10%; my GDP-weighted estimate of ~2.8-3.1% is NOT published and is the weakest link in my CQ2. Falsifier: the GDP-weighted measure printing below 2.0% for four consecutive quarters.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ2 falls from 43 to roughly 20 (the arithmetic head-start on the 2027-36 average evaporates). CQ6 shifts hard: C from 22 to ~10, D from 22 to ~33, B roughly unchanged. Hypothesis plank (c) rises from 33 to ~60. CQ4 P50 falls ~0.3pp toward 1.9% as the inflation-risk component of the term premium deflates.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Internationally educated nurse THROUGHPUT, not stock: annual NCLEX-RN first-time pass counts for in